## Why this article exists

Most men walk into a barber holding a phone with a picture of someone whose hair type, growth direction, and skull shape have almost nothing in common with theirs. The result is a cut that fights the hair instead of working with it: it falls wrong, sticks up where you don't want it, and never quite sits. The cut on the screen worked on that person because their biomechanics supported it. Yours don't, and no amount of pomade fixes it.

The fix is to do the opposite. Map your *own* biomechanics first (type, direction, skull shape), and then choose a cut that works *with* what you have. ==The Blueprint Cut is not a specific style. It is the cut your biology will support without you fighting it every morning, that frames the face you sharpened, and that lightly counter-balances whatever asymmetry your face carries.==

