## A. Hair type biomechanics

The standard classification is the **Andre Walker scale**[^1]: four categories, each with three sub-types based on curl pattern. The relevance to a cut is not vanity; ==it is the physical behavior of each strand under gravity, humidity, and product.==

| Type | Description | Mechanical behavior | Cuts that work | Cuts that fight it |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **1A to 1C: Straight** | Round follicle, no curl. 1A fine, 1C coarse. | Falls flat, follows gravity, holds a line. Reflects light cleanly. | Side parts, low fades with length on top, classic taper, slick-back, longer fringe. | Heavy texturized crops that need lift (straight hair won't hold the lift without daily product). |
| **2A to 2C: Wavy** | Slightly oval follicle. Hair forms an S-shape. 2A loose, 2C defined. | Holds shape with light product. Has natural texture. Reacts to humidity. | Textured crops, medium-length tousled, mid-length with movement, fringe with bend. | Pin-sharp slicked styles (wavy hair fights against being flattened, lifts within an hour). |
| **3A to 3C: Curly** | Elliptical follicle. Defined spiral. 3A loose curl, 3C tight coil. | Stands up and out, not down. Volume comes from the curl pattern itself. | High-top curl-out, taper-fade with curl left on top, low-and-loose afro-adjacent shapes. | Anything requiring the hair to *lie* flat. Forced flattening damages the curl pattern long-term. |
| **4A to 4C: Coily / Kinky** | Tight zig-zag follicle. 4A defined coil, 4C tightest. | Maximum natural volume. Shrinks dramatically when dry. | Defined fades with shape sculpted out of the coil, twists, locs, sponge styles. | Anything that ignores shrinkage geometry: what looks long damp is half that length dry. |

> [!tip] Malaysian-context reality check
> ==Most Malaysian men are 1A to 2B.== Predominantly straight hair, sometimes with a wave through it. If you've been hate-following Western YouTube barbers chasing pin-curl-out high-volume textured looks designed for 2C to 3A hair, you've been fighting biology for years. Cuts that work with straight or lightly-wavy Asian hair are different, and there are excellent local references (Mr Barber, Truefitt & Hill KL, JustBarber chain) who cut for this hair type natively. Use them.

### Density and shaft diameter

Type tells you the curl pattern. Two more variables matter:

- **Density:** how many hairs per cm². High density means full visual coverage, supports more shape. Low density needs cuts that don't expose the scalp lines.
- **Shaft diameter:** fine, medium, or coarse. Fine hair has no body; coarse hair has natural body but resists shaping.

If you ran a Tier 2 or Tier 3 scan in the Hair series ([[Part 1.1 - What Actually Matters For Your Hair|Hair Part 1.1]]), you already have these numbers. Use them: they are the same numbers the cut depends on.

