## B. Growth direction, whorls, and cowlicks

Each hair grows out of the scalp at a specific angle, and across the scalp those angles are organized into a **growth pattern** centred on one or more **whorls** (the spiral pattern, usually on the crown).[^2] Roughly **94% of men have a single clockwise whorl on the crown**; the rest have either a counter-clockwise whorl, a double whorl, or an offset pattern.[^3]

The whorl matters because every cut either works with the growth direction or fights it. ==The patches where the hair refuses to lie the way you part it are not a styling failure; they are your growth pattern asserting itself.==

> [!check] Mapping your own scalp: 5-minute audit
> 1. Wet your hair fully. Don't towel-dry; let gravity show you the natural fall.
> 2. **Crown whorl:** look at the top of your head in two mirrors or with your phone in selfie video. Identify where the spiral starts and which direction it rotates. This is your dominant whorl.
> 3. **Cowlicks:** look at the hairline (forehead, temples, nape). Any patch where the hair stands up instead of lying down is a cowlick. Note the exact location and direction.
> 4. **Crown opening:** if the whorl produces a visible "swirl" or partial opening at the centre, note its size. A large open whorl is harder to disguise with length.
> 5. **Front growth angle:** does your front hairline grow straight forward, or does it have a left or right lean? This dictates whether your natural part falls left, right, or centre.

The whorl direction, cowlick locations, and front growth angle are **non-negotiable**. You design the cut around them, not against them. A side part fights a centre whorl. A swept-up front fights a forward-growing cowlick. A flat-on-top look fights a high crown whorl with vertical lift. ==Map first, choose second.==

