## C. Skull and face geometry: what silhouettes work

This section moves from biology (type, direction) into convention (silhouette aesthetics). The convention is genuinely useful (drawn from a few hundred years of hairdressing observation and from facial-recognition research on perceived attractiveness ratings), but ==it is convention, not law.== Treat the table as a strong default, not a rule.[^4]

The two axes that matter most:

- **Face shape:** oval, round, square, oblong, heart, diamond.
- **Skull shape from above:** round (front-back ≈ side-side), elongated (front-back > side-side), or flat (e.g. flat occiput, common in some East Asian populations).

| Face shape | Goal | Cuts that flatter | Cuts to avoid |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Oval** | Maintain proportion (the "default" canvas). | Almost anything. Side parts, textured crops, mid-length, classic short-back-and-sides. | Extreme width at the temples that turns oval into round. |
| **Round** | Add vertical length; reduce horizontal width. | Lift on top, taper or fade at the sides, side parts not centre parts, slight forward fringe asymmetry. | Bowl cuts, blunt straight-across fringes, flat tops with bulk at the sides. |
| **Square** | Soften the jaw without losing structure. | Textured crops with some movement on top, side-swept fringes, medium-length wavy. | Sharp military buzz that doubles down on the square. |
| **Oblong / long** | Add horizontal width; reduce vertical length. | Fringes and forward-swept fronts, fuller sides (not extreme but not buzzed), medium length. | Pompadours, slick-backs with extreme vertical lift, very short sides. |
| **Heart** | Balance a wider forehead with the narrower chin. | Side-swept fringe, mid-length with volume at the jaw line, textured medium length. | Centre parts that frame the forehead width directly. |
| **Diamond** | Soften the cheekbone width; add at the temples. | Fringes, length around the temples, swept-forward fronts. | Buzz cuts that expose every angle of the diamond. |

The **skull shape from above** then modifies the side-and-back work:

- **Round skull:** most fades work well; the back is naturally curved.
- **Elongated skull:** keep the back length short; long hair at the nape elongates further.
- **Flat occiput** (the flat back-of-head common in some Asian populations): leave a touch more length and bulk at the back to round it out. ==This is the single most under-served point in generic Western barber advice and matters a lot for Asian male cuts.==[^5]

