## D. The Blueprint Cut: balancing facial asymmetry

==Every face is asymmetric.== Most men's faces lean a few millimetres or degrees off centre: one cheekbone higher, one eye slightly smaller, one side of the jaw fuller. Plastic surgeons document this routinely with overlay imaging. A Blueprint Cut is the cut that **lightly counter-balances** that natural asymmetry without trying to hide it.[^6]

The principle: ==add visual weight to the side that needs lifting; reduce visual weight where the face is already fuller.== This is the same logic clothing uses (vertical lines lengthen, horizontal widen) applied to the hair.

> [!check] The 4-step Blueprint Cut audit
> 1. **Take a clean centred selfie** under flat overhead light, face neutral. Don't smile. Don't tilt.
> 2. **Mirror-flip the image** in your phone (most editing apps have a "flip horizontal"). Compare the original and the flip. The differences you see (eye height, cheek fullness, jaw squareness) are the asymmetries to counter.
> 3. **Identify the dominant lean.** Which side is "stronger" (broader, more angular, fuller)? Which is "softer" (rounder, smaller, recessed)?
> 4. **Bias the cut to the softer side.** Add a touch more length, more volume, or a swept-over fringe that adds visual mass to the softer side. The stronger side stays cleaner and tighter.

> [!tip] A worked example
> If the **left side** of your face is fuller (rounder cheek, fuller jaw) and the **right side** is softer (flatter cheek, recessed jaw), part the hair on the **left**. The part falling on the fuller side means the longer length sweeps *over* toward the softer right side, adding mass there. The result is not "fixing" anything: both sides are still there. The cut just stops the asymmetry from being the first thing the eye lands on.

> [!warning] Don't overcorrect
> The goal is *light* counter-balance, not radical concealment. Heavy comb-overs, dramatic side-sweeps, or asymmetric razor work read as trying too hard and pull more attention to the asymmetry than they cover. ==1 to 2 cm of weight difference is enough.==

