## E. What to tell a barber

The bridge from biology to chair is **vocabulary**. ==Most bad cuts happen because the customer described what they wanted ambiguously and the barber filled the gap with their own default.== Eliminate the ambiguity.

> [!check] The five-line barber script
> 1. **Hair type and growth notes:** "I have type 1B straight hair with a crown whorl on the right and a small cowlick at the front-right."
> 2. **The silhouette:** describe the *shape*, not a celebrity. E.g. "Short on the sides, longer on top, with the top swept to the left." Or: "Mid-fade up the sides, textured crop on top with a forward fringe."
> 3. **The numbers:** clipper guard sizes for the sides (1 to 4), and an *approximate length in centimetres* for the top (e.g. "4 cm on top, 1 cm at the temples, blended"). Numbers remove interpretation.
> 4. **The part / weight bias:** "Part on the left. More length on the right side so it sweeps over." Or "No part, brushed forward."
> 5. **What you definitely don't want:** name 1 to 2 specific things. "Don't take the sides shorter than guard 1." "Don't taper into the neck; keep the back square."

> [!tip] On reference photos
> ==Bring reference photos only of people with similar hair type, density, and face shape to yours.== Showing a photo of someone with thick wavy 2B hair when yours is fine 1A hair guarantees a cut that won't sit. If you must use references, bring two: one for the silhouette you want, one of yourself with hair the length you want as a calibration baseline.

### Finding the right barber in Malaysia

- **Asian-hair specialists:** TBK Barbershop, JustBarber, Truefitt & Hill (KL, Pavilion / Bangsar), The Cut Barber chain. They cut straight 1A to 2B hair natively without trying to force texture into it.
- **What to test first visit:** ask for a 0.5 cm trim at the front and sides only, with the same silhouette as your current cut. ==Never get a full restyle on the first visit with a new barber.== If the 0.5 cm trim is precise, then go full restyle next time.
- **Price band:** RM 25 to 50 at neighbourhood barbers, RM 60 to 120 at mid-tier (TBK, JustBarber), RM 150 to 300 at Truefitt-tier salons. The price-to-quality curve flattens hard above RM 120 for daily cuts.

