## Part 3.0 Takeaways

> [!check] Key concepts to internalize
> - **Styling lives here; retention lives in the [[Part 1.1 - What Actually Matters For Your Hair|Hair folder]].** Read both; they feed each other.
> - **Hair type dictates which cuts your biology will *hold*.** Most Malaysian men are 1A to 2B; cuts designed for 2C to 3A textured hair won't sit on you.
> - **Map your whorls, cowlicks, and front growth angle before choosing a cut.** The patches that won't lie down are the biology dictating the design.
> - **Skull shape modifies the back.** Flat occiputs (common in Asian men) need a touch more length at the back; this is under-served in generic barber advice.
> - **The Blueprint Cut lightly counter-balances asymmetry:** add weight to the softer side, keep the stronger side cleaner. Light correction, not concealment.
> - **The 5-line script removes ambiguity at the chair.** Type, silhouette, numbers, part, no-go list.
> - **The 7-day mirror test is the only KPI.** If it doesn't fall right on day 7 without product, the cut is fighting you.

## Your Task List

1. **Map your biomechanics.** Wet your hair, take phone-camera shots from above (crown) and from the front (hairline). Note whorl direction, cowlicks, front growth angle.
2. **Audit your face shape.** Clean front selfie under flat light, mirror-flip in your phone, identify your dominant face shape and your asymmetry lean.
3. **Pick a barber by hair type fit, not price.** Book a 0.5 cm trim with the candidate barber as the test cut. Full restyle only after one successful trim.
4. **Write your 5-line script.** Save it as a note on your phone. Walk in with it on every visit.
5. **Re-run the 7-day mirror test after every new cut.** If it fails, log what fell wrong before the next visit so the adjustment is specific.
6. **Cross-check the Hair folder.** If your front hairline or crown is moving (Norwood progression), read [[Part 1.1 - What Actually Matters For Your Hair|Hair Part 1.1]] before locking in any cut: retention strategy may dictate length choices.

