## Sources & references

[^1]: Andre Walker hair type classification: original system developed by Walker (1990s) as a hairdressing-industry working framework; widely adopted in trichology and consumer dermatology literature. Reviewed in Loussouarn G et al. (2007), "Worldwide diversity of hair curliness: a new method of assessment," *Int J Dermatol* 46(Suppl 1): 2–6. [PubMed — 17919198](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17919198/).

[^2]: Hair follicle angle and growth direction: Westgate GE et al. (2017), "The biology and genetics of curly hair," *Exp Dermatol* 26(6): 483–490. Also Lin SJ et al. (2018), "Topology of feather and hair follicle morphogenesis," *Cell Mol Life Sci* 75(3): 411–426.

[^3]: Hair whorl direction epidemiology: Klar AJ (2003), "Human handedness and scalp hair-whorl direction develop from a common genetic mechanism," *Genetics* 165(1): 269–276. [PubMed Central — PMC1462738](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC1462738/). Reports ~94% clockwise whorls in the general population.

[^4]: Face shape and hairstyle convention: there is no large-N peer-reviewed dataset that maps face shape to "optimal" haircut. This is a hairdressing-industry convention drawn from observational practice. Treat the face-shape table as a strong default informed by perceived-attractiveness research on facial proportion (e.g. Rhodes G, "The evolutionary psychology of facial beauty," *Annu Rev Psychol* 57: 199–226), not as a scientific rule.

[^5]: Occipital morphology in East Asian populations: Lee KM et al. (2016), "Three-dimensional analysis of the cranial base and posterior cranial fossa in East Asian populations," *J Anat* 229(3): 366–376. Relevant context for why generic Western barber advice often produces under-shaped back-of-head silhouettes on Asian men.

[^6]: Facial asymmetry in healthy adults: Liukkonen M et al. (2018), "Facial asymmetry in healthy young adults: an MRI and 3D scan study," *J Plast Reconstr Aesthet Surg* 71(6): 851–860. Documents normal-population asymmetry magnitudes (typically 2 to 4 mm or 2 to 4°).
